NNAAMMEE
       smtpd - Postfix SMTP server

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       ssmmttppdd [generic Postfix daemon options]

       sseennddmmaaiill --bbss

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The  SMTP  server accepts network connection requests and performs zero
       or more SMTP transactions per connection.   Each  received  message  is
       piped  through  the  cclleeaannuupp(8) daemon, and is placed into the iinnccoommiinngg
       queue as one single queue file.  For this mode of operation,  the  pro‐
       gram expects to be run from the mmaasstteerr(8) process manager.

       Alternatively,  the SMTP server be can run in stand-alone mode; this is
       traditionally obtained with "sseennddmmaaiill --bbss".  When the SMTP server  runs
       stand-alone  with  non  $mmaaiill__oowwnneerr  privileges,  it receives mail even
       while the mail system is not running, deposits messages  directly  into
       the  mmaaiillddrroopp queue, and disables the SMTP server’s access policies. As
       of Postfix version 2.3, the SMTP server refuses to  receive  mail  from
       the network when it runs with non $mmaaiill__oowwnneerr privileges.

       The  SMTP  server  implements  a variety of policies for connection re‐
       quests, and for parameters given to HHEELLOO,, EETTRRNN,,  MMAAIILL  FFRROOMM,,  VVRRFFYY  and
       RRCCPPTT TTOO commands. They are detailed below and in the mmaaiinn..ccff configura‐
       tion file.

SSEECCUURRIITTYY
       The  SMTP  server  is  moderately  security-sensitive. It talks to SMTP
       clients and to DNS servers on the network. The SMTP server can  be  run
       chrooted at fixed low privilege.

